#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:5
#: 9b42c9ec51084e848f148db632eea95e
msgid "ESC Telemetry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:9
#: f532704b65b648fea15fc3c0fc3da7ec
msgid "If the ESC has this capability, it allows monitoring and logging of performance data that previously required additional sensors (like power modules and RPM sensors). The detailed data provided by every ESC allows real-time decisions and individual ESC or motor performance tuning and failure analysis. Note that a given ESC may or may not have a specific sensor type's data transmitted via telemetry. It is common for 4 in 1 escs to provide voltage and current sensors but not transmit the data via telemetry, but rather by direct connection to the autopilot. Check the ESC data sheet and connection information for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:11
#: abe0527629664913bb87bc93e5a4fb44
msgid "Telemetry data may be conveyed to the autopilot either by a separate wire connection to an autopilot's UART RX pin or across the signal wire used to convey motor speed information (Bi-Directional DShot) or CAN messages for CAN based ESCs. For non-CAN ESCs, this capability is available primarily in selected BLHeli ESCs running stock or BlueJay firmware. But it is possible for an ESC not to implement the BLHeli firmware and still provide this capability. Currently, ArduPilot only supports this for BLHeli/BlueJay telemetry compatible ESCs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:13
#: 9a1dbe3370214980b7fc4eea6b68c136
msgid "ESC telemetry of motor RPM is especially useful for controlling the center frequency of harmonic notch filters to control noise. See :ref:`common-esc-telem-based-notch` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:15
#: 0ed9840ffbb74aef802478907a58810b
msgid "ArduPilot does not currently support the polling of the ESCs for telemetry data via throttle idle messages over the signal line in non DShot protocols."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:18
#: 9aacd94b407140a89201c3970064cd0c
msgid "Connecting the ESCs Telemetry wire"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:24
#: 393a6030f808489ead217d0355d9a049
msgid "Connect all ESC telemetry wires to a single serial port's RX pin on the autopilot (above diagram uses Serial5 as an example).  A pin or wire for ESC telemetry is pre-soldered on most BLHeli32 ESCs. If the wire isn't pre-soldered you will need to solder it yourself. CubePilot serial port pinsouts can be found :ref:`here <common-thecube-overview>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:26
#: c0f0b4d6e1c64197aa70726a8a5b690d
msgid "Set the following parameters to enable BLHeli32/AM32 telemetry feedback to the autopilot's serial port:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:28
#: 6e14cc6032cd4e7db7de84bf6f8625f0
msgid ":ref:`SERIALx_PROTOCOL <SERIAL5_PROTOCOL>` 16 (= ESC telemetry) where \"x\" is the autopilot serial port number connected to the ESCs telemetry wire.  The mapping between serial port numbering and UART physical ports for you autopilot should be documented in its description page linked :ref:`here <common-autopilots>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:30
#: f3339120adcc4c80a568615b37cec86c
msgid ":ref:`SERVO_BLH_TRATE <SERVO_BLH_TRATE>` defaults to 10 and normally does not need to be changed. this enables telemetry at a 10Hz update rate from the ESC.  If using the :ref:`harmonic notch feature <common-imu-notch-filtering>` this can be raised to 100."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:32
#: 93cc2d1ac00743df9086d9cac0214534
msgid ":ref:`SERVO_BLH_POLES <SERVO_BLH_POLES>` defaults to 14 which applies to the majority of brushless motors and normally does not need to be changed.  Adjust as required if you're using motors with a pole count other than 14 to calculate true motor shaft RPM from ESC's e-field RPM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:34
#: b48b3051395247f5a912ef6d07fa3f32
msgid "using the RPM value reported using single wire telemetry for the center frequency adjustment of the :ref:`harmonic notch feature <common-imu-notch-filtering>`  works well, but the responsiveness is slower than using telemetry provided by bi-directional DShot. See next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:39
#: e14abc8bec8646499a5cf68f01e5036e
msgid "Bi-directional DShot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:41
#: af2aa04312e2400abc02ede29c66bc82
msgid "Newer versions of AM32, BLHeli32 (32.7 and higher), and BLHeli_S (16.73 and higher) support returning motor RPM values over the DShot signal line. Supporting bi-directional DShot requires exclusive use of one or more DMA channels and thus not all autopilots support it. Versions that support bi-directional DShot have this stated in their wiki pages, see :ref:`common-autopilots` for your autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:43
#: db4081bb45df4946a49149aead5667da
msgid "Some autopilots with IOMCUs can not only support Dshot on their \"Main\" outputs (see :ref:`common-dshot-escs` for setup and more information), but also bi-directional DShot on their first four outputs. Currently, this is limited only to Pixhawk6X/C autopilots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:46
#: ca8c9738093c413193a3179f218d384b
msgid "Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:48
#: 25b4bd0620b54322886a701390251e86
msgid "First ensure that you have an appropriate version of BLHeli32 or BLHeli_S installed on your ESCs. The majority of ESCs do not come pre-installed with these versions. The official 32.7 version of BLHeli32 supports bi-directional DShot. Official versions of BLHeli_S do not support bi-directional DShot, you will need to either buy a version from `BLHeli_S JESC <https://jflight.net/index.php?route=common/home&language=en-gb>`__ or use `BLHeli_S BlueJay <https://github.com/bird-sanctuary/bluejay/>`__ . If you try and enable bi-directional DShot with the wrong firmware version then unpredictable motor operation can occur. ESC RPM telemetry is especially useful for controlling the center frequencies of harmonic notch noise filters, see :ref:`common-esc-telem-based-notch`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:54
#: f0fc344e4d2a46d7842be9bb52a6c925
msgid "Set the following parameters to enable BLHeli32 and BLHeli_S bi-directional DShot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:56
#: 46288abb015d47edb7dfecb0a77fb89d
msgid ":ref:`SERVO_BLH_BDMASK <SERVO_BLH_BDMASK>`: a bitmap used to enable BLHeli32, AM32, or BLHeli_S bi-directional DShot support. On autopilots without IOMCU this would normally be set to 15 to indicate four active channels. On autopilots with an IOMCU this can be set to 3840 to indicate four active AUX channels (bi-directional DShot will only work on the AUX outputs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:58
#: c44f18e352e64e188fdf1a25192f47b4
msgid ":ref:`SERVO_BLH_POLES <SERVO_BLH_POLES>` defaults to 14 which applies to the majority of brushless motors and normally does not need to be changed. Adjust as required if you're using motors with a pole count other than 14 to calculate true motor shaft RPM from ESC's e-field RPM (small motors might have 12 poles)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:60
#: 4900765f052d4089b1d2df2bdc79cdd9
msgid ":ref:`SERVO_DSHOT_ESC<SERVO_DSHOT_ESC>` must be set to the type of Dshot ESC you are using."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:63
#: 84994b2182d24c39b1f6e54401a351b0
msgid "ESC Telemetry Logging and Reporting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:65
#: 547f7f06d48e4cff98eace15d68ca881
msgid "The autopilot requests status information from one ESC at a time, cycling between them. This information is logged to the onboard log's ESCn messages and can be viewed in any :ref:`ArduPilot compatible log viewer <common-logs>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:67
#: 02a468d9950d4d0e8b265dded2fedc53
msgid "RPM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:68
#: 171811c8f6e44d14931ea1a71c303f3c
msgid "Voltage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:69
#: ada6d551b0b643eaa6cb97db3441aae6
msgid "Current"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:70
#: 2bd2ce90f19a4fa5ba3bb97925aeef7f
msgid "Temperature"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:71
#: 15c2dcab18fb45b79a2461a33d9d39c4
msgid "Total Current"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:73
#: 3c6dca22946f4034b4c2f84450952e93
msgid "The RCOU messages are also written to the onboard logs which hold the requested output level sent to the ESCs expressed as a number from 1000 (meaning stopped) to 2000 (meaning full output)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:75
#: b4e96fd838ad49bf9594f35fe878f832
msgid "This data can also be viewed in real-time using a ground station.  If using the Mission Planner go to the Flight Data screen's status tab and look for esc1_rpm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:83
#: 9b45e76af7dc473bba03722ccacbcb71
msgid "Sending BLHeli32 telemetry data to the GCS requires the telemetry connection use MAVLink2.  ArduPilot uses MAVLink2 by default on the USB port but if another port is used it may be necessary to set the SERIALx_PROTOCOL parameter to 2 (where \"x\" is the serial port number used for the telemetry connection)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:85
#: 157c66f26e2449eeb0db140b56f7a4d5
msgid "In addition, some telemetry values can be displayed on the integrated :ref:`on-board OSD <common-osd-overview>`, if your autopilot has one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:87
#: caeafd9e74f84873a670ee56d5f8558e
msgid "Newer firmware versions (such as BlHeli32 starting at version 32.10, and Bluejay starting at version 0.19) report some of the information above also via bi-directional DShot, using a protocol extension known as `Extended DShot Telemetry <https://github.com/bird-sanctuary/extended-dshot-telemetry>`__. The version 2 of this protocol, EDTv2, also specifies the following information that can be reported by an ESC:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:89
#: bd82f39252a74bda86fe6697cf2989c1
msgid "Commutation stress"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:90
#: 0011c92d4b60478ab255ed354ed9806e
msgid "Error events (such as propeller stalls)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:91
#: 4cbe57a53310481b8749fb16cf1e6d80
msgid "Warning events (such as desyncs)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:92
#: 699704f9804949359af155341db0f428
msgid "Alert events (such as demagnetization events)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:94
#: dae8e659ca8f4f9d93eda87ff468bb28
msgid "If an ESC connected via bidirectional DShot supports this extension, this information is automatically sent to the flight controller, where it is logged as EDT2 messages. This information is typically sent on a best-efforts basis, and is intended mainly for post-flight analysis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:99
#: 4de5f4280c094a11b142d88220d73e81
msgid "Use as Battery Monitor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-telemetry.rst:101
#: fb41eabcb0514dcc9800a856ff387a50
msgid "By setting a battery monitor instance to BLHeli32 ESC type (for example :ref:`BATT2_MONITOR<BATT2_MONITOR>` = 9), all connected BLHeli32 ESCs with connected telemetry wiring to the configured autopilot serial port, will be aggregated as a single source. Normally all the ESC's reported voltages and temperatures reported will be averaged, their currents totaled, and the consumed current accumulated. However, an individual monitor may select which ESC's values will be used, instead of all, by setting (Battery Monitor 3 will used for example here) :ref:`BATT3_ESC_MASK<BATT3_ESC_MASK>` bits for the indexes of which ESC's values will be used. Default is to use all ESCs setup to report power telemetry."
msgstr ""
